{"authored" [{"question" "q-338921" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+25\\lambda y=0,\\,{y}^{′}(0)={y}^{′}(\\pi )=0,\\,0\\le x\\le \\pi ,\\,\\lambda \\ge 0.\n$$\n\nFind the parameter values corresponding to the eigenvalues and the associated eigenfunctions of this problem.$\\,$ The eigenvalues correspond to the parameter values ${\\lambda }_{n}=$ {{field-1}}, where $n=0,1,2,\\ldots.$\n\n$\\,$ The eigenfunctions are ${y}_{n}(x)=$ {{field-2}}, where ${B}_{n}\\in R\\setminus\\{0\\}.$" "category" "mathematical_correction" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-338921/review.json" "file_sha256" "47f4cc989168be620ead3ebf2015ec162a45de7646242ce35e832455f9b01a19" "transaction" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-338921/transaction.edn" "transaction_sha256" "6419e299559d3331ec1cd7cc2262179600549184be180abbcf01e76ee49d8075" "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-338921/verification.json" "basis" 2615 "rationale" "The Neumann problem y''+25*lambda*y=0 on [0,pi] admits nonzero constants at lambda=0. At lambda>0 its derivative conditions select cosine and force 5*sqrt(lambda)=n for positive integers. The source correctly grades n^2/25 and B_n*cos(5*x*sqrt(lambda_n)), and its revealed worked solution explicitly confirms the zero eigenvalue. The original N convention remains unspecified and B_n=0 would be trivial. Make only the indexing and nonzero amplitudes explicit in the study prompt and append the complete-spectrum note to the unchanged authentic worked solution. The original source/rendering, predictions, grade, options and metadata remain preserved. No N convention is attributed to the source author."} {"question" "q-338921" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We consider the cases $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda >0$ separately, since they correspond to different solutions. \n\n**Case 1**: First, we consider $\\lambda =0.$ \n\nSetting $\\lambda =0$ gives the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny=Ax+B.\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions ${y}^{′}(0)={y}^{′}(\\pi )=0$ yields $A=0.$ Thus, we have a nontrivial solution, so $\\lambda =0$ is a parameter corresponding to the eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider $\\lambda >0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}+25\\lambda  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $m=\\pm 5\\text{i}\\sqrt{\\lambda }.$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x\\sqrt{\\lambda }),\\,{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x\\sqrt{\\lambda })\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x\\sqrt{\\lambda })+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x\\sqrt{\\lambda }).\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-5A\\sqrt{\\lambda }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x\\sqrt{\\lambda })+5B\\sqrt{\\lambda }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x\\sqrt{\\lambda }).\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition ${y}^{′}(0)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n-5A\\sqrt{\\lambda }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+5B\\sqrt{\\lambda }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)=0\n$$\n\nwhich gives $B=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x\\sqrt{\\lambda }).\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition ${y}^{′}(\\pi )=0$ yields\n\n$$\n-5A\\sqrt{\\lambda }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\pi \\sqrt{\\lambda })=0.\n$$\n\nFor nontrivial solutions, we require \n\n$$\n5\\pi \\sqrt{\\lambda }=n\\pi \n$$\n\nfor $n\\in N.$ This gives the parameter values corresponding to the eigenvalues:\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{n}=\\frac{{n}^{2}}{25}.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding eigenfunctions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{n}(x)={B}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{n}}),\\,{B}_{n}\\in R.\n$$\n\nIndexing and nonzero-amplitude clarification: In the positive-eigenvalue case above, $n=1,2,3,\\ldots$. The separate zero case contributes $\\lambda_0=0$ with $y_0(x)=B_0$, where $B_0\\ne0$. Thus the complete spectrum and eigenfunctions are $\\lambda_n=n^2/25$ and $y_n(x)=B_n\\cos(nx)$ for $n=0,1,2,\\ldots$ and $B_n\\ne0$. Since $5\\sqrt{\\lambda_n}=n$ for these indices, this is exactly the accepted displayed formula $B_n\\cos(5x\\sqrt{\\lambda_n})$. These explicit indices avoid assuming a convention for the source symbol $\\mathbb N$; a zero amplitude would produce the trivial solution, which is not an eigenfunction." "category" "mathematical_correction" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-338921/review.json" "file_sha256" "47f4cc989168be620ead3ebf2015ec162a45de7646242ce35e832455f9b01a19" "transaction" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-338921/transaction.edn" "transaction_sha256" "6419e299559d3331ec1cd7cc2262179600549184be180abbcf01e76ee49d8075" "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-338921/verification.json" "basis" 2615 "rationale" "The Neumann problem y''+25*lambda*y=0 on [0,pi] admits nonzero constants at lambda=0. At lambda>0 its derivative conditions select cosine and force 5*sqrt(lambda)=n for positive integers. The source correctly grades n^2/25 and B_n*cos(5*x*sqrt(lambda_n)), and its revealed worked solution explicitly confirms the zero eigenvalue. The original N convention remains unspecified and B_n=0 would be trivial. Make only the indexing and nonzero amplitudes explicit in the study prompt and append the complete-spectrum note to the unchanged authentic worked solution. The original source/rendering, predictions, grade, options and metadata remain preserved. No N convention is attributed to the source author."} {"question" "q-338921" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{{n}^{2}}{25}"] "category" "mathematical_correction" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-338921/review.json" "file_sha256" "47f4cc989168be620ead3ebf2015ec162a45de7646242ce35e832455f9b01a19" "transaction" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-338921/transaction.edn" "transaction_sha256" "6419e299559d3331ec1cd7cc2262179600549184be180abbcf01e76ee49d8075" "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-338921/verification.json" "basis" 2615 "rationale" "The Neumann problem y''+25*lambda*y=0 on [0,pi] admits nonzero constants at lambda=0. At lambda>0 its derivative conditions select cosine and force 5*sqrt(lambda)=n for positive integers. The source correctly grades n^2/25 and B_n*cos(5*x*sqrt(lambda_n)), and its revealed worked solution explicitly confirms the zero eigenvalue. The original N convention remains unspecified and B_n=0 would be trivial. Make only the indexing and nonzero amplitudes explicit in the study prompt and append the complete-spectrum note to the unchanged authentic worked solution. The original source/rendering, predictions, grade, options and metadata remain preserved. No N convention is attributed to the source author."} {"question" "q-338921" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{n}})"] "category" "mathematical_correction" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-338921/review.json" "file_sha256" "47f4cc989168be620ead3ebf2015ec162a45de7646242ce35e832455f9b01a19" "transaction" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-338921/transaction.edn" "transaction_sha256" "6419e299559d3331ec1cd7cc2262179600549184be180abbcf01e76ee49d8075" "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-338921/verification.json" "basis" 2615 "rationale" "The Neumann problem y''+25*lambda*y=0 on [0,pi] admits nonzero constants at lambda=0. At lambda>0 its derivative conditions select cosine and force 5*sqrt(lambda)=n for positive integers. The source correctly grades n^2/25 and B_n*cos(5*x*sqrt(lambda_n)), and its revealed worked solution explicitly confirms the zero eigenvalue. The original N convention remains unspecified and B_n=0 would be trivial. Make only the indexing and nonzero amplitudes explicit in the study prompt and append the complete-spectrum note to the unchanged authentic worked solution. The original source/rendering, predictions, grade, options and metadata remain preserved. No N convention is attributed to the source author."} {"question" "q-338729" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-8\\lambda {y}^{′}+16{\\lambda }^{2}y=0,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=0,\\,y(2)+2{y}^{′}(2)=0,\\,1\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\lambda \\in R.\n$$\n\nFind the eigenvalues and eigenfunctions of this problem. Assume that $B$ and $C$ are constants.\n$\\,$ The eigenvalues are $\\lambda =$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The eigenfunctions are $y=$ {{field-2}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071917/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5cae1fd22b1e18b4461c39cf1037217cd3796498a0960b0386be8472969e06a" "basis" 2612 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071917/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-338729" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We consider the cases $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda \\ne 0$ separately, since they correspond to different solutions. \n\n**Case 1**: First, we consider $\\lambda =0.$ \n\nSetting $\\lambda =0$ gives the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny=Ax+B.\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions ${y}^{′}(1)=0$ and $y(2)+2{y}^{′}(2)=0$ yields the trivial solution $y≡0.$ Since the zero solution is not an eigenfunction, $\\lambda =0$ is not an eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider $\\lambda \\ne 0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}-8\\lambda m+16{\\lambda }^{2} & =0 \\\\ (m-4\\lambda )(m-4\\lambda ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the double root $m=4\\lambda .$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{4\\lambda x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)=x{e}^{4\\lambda x}\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{4\\lambda x}+Bx{e}^{4\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =4A\\lambda {e}^{4\\lambda x}+B{e}^{4\\lambda x}+4B\\lambda x{e}^{4\\lambda x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition ${y}^{′}(1)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4A\\lambda {e}^{4\\lambda }+B{e}^{4\\lambda }+4B\\lambda {e}^{4\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ 4A\\lambda +B+4B\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ (4\\lambda )A+(1+4\\lambda )B & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(2)+2{y}^{′}(2)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{8\\lambda }+2B{e}^{8\\lambda }+2(4A\\lambda {e}^{8\\lambda }+B{e}^{8\\lambda }+8B\\lambda {e}^{8\\lambda }) & =0 \\\\ A+2B+2(4A\\lambda +B+8B\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ A+2B+8A\\lambda +2B+16B\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ (1+8\\lambda )A+(4+16\\lambda )B & =0 \\\\ (1+8\\lambda )A+4(1+4\\lambda )B & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following systems of equations for $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(4\\lambda )A+(1+4\\lambda )B=0 \\\\ (1+8\\lambda )A+4(1+4\\lambda )B=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis homogeneous linear system has a nontrivial solution if and only if the determinant is zero. This gives\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}4\\lambda  & 1+4\\lambda  \\\\ 1+8\\lambda  & 4(1+4\\lambda )\\end{aligned}|=0.\n$$\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\lambda \\cdot 4(1+4\\lambda )-(1+4\\lambda )(1+8\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ (1+4\\lambda )[16\\lambda -(1+8\\lambda )] & =0 \\\\ (1+4\\lambda )[16\\lambda -1-8\\lambda ] & =0 \\\\ (1+4\\lambda )(8\\lambda -1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-\\frac{1}{4}\\text{ and }{\\lambda }_{2}=\\frac{1}{8}.$\n\nFinally, we find the eigenfunction corresponding to each eigenvalue:\n\n- Consider ${\\lambda }_{1}=-\\frac{1}{4}.$ Then, we have the solution\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}(x) & =A{e}^{4{\\lambda }_{1}x}+Bx{e}^{4{\\lambda }_{1}x} \\\\  & =A{e}^{-x}+Bx{e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting ${\\lambda }_{1}$ into\n\n$$\n(4\\lambda )A+(1+4\\lambda )B=0\n$$\n\n \nwe get \n\n$$\n-A+(1-1)B=0\n$$\n\n \nwhich gives $A=0,$ and where $B$ is a free parameter. Thus, the eigenfunction corresponding to this eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=Bx{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=\\frac{1}{8},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}(x) & =A{e}^{4{\\lambda }_{2}x}+Bx{e}^{4{\\lambda }_{2}x} \\\\  & =A{e}^{x/2}+Bx{e}^{x/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting ${\\lambda }_{2}$ into\n\n$$\n(4\\lambda )A+(1+4\\lambda )B=0\n$$\n\n \nwe get \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}A+\\frac{3}{2}B=0\\,⇒\\,A=-3B\n$$\n\nwhere $B$ is a free parameter. Thus, the eigenfunction corresponding to this eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}(x) & =-3B{e}^{x/2}+Bx{e}^{x/2} \\\\  & =B{e}^{x/2}(x-3) \\\\  & =C{e}^{x/2}(x-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we have set $C=B.$\n\nSo, the eigenfunctions corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively, are $Bx{e}^{-x}\\text{ and }C{e}^{x/2}(x-3).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071917/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5cae1fd22b1e18b4461c39cf1037217cd3796498a0960b0386be8472969e06a" "basis" 2612 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071917/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-338729" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Cours