thacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071917/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5cae1fd22b1e18b4461c39cf1037217cd3796498a0960b0386be8472969e06a" "basis" 2612 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071917/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-338853" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "$Bx{e}^{-7x/6}$ only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071917/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5cae1fd22b1e18b4461c39cf1037217cd3796498a0960b0386be8472969e06a" "basis" 2612 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071917/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-338853" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "$Bx{e}^{-x}$ only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071917/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5cae1fd22b1e18b4461c39cf1037217cd3796498a0960b0386be8472969e06a" "basis" 2612 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071917/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-338853" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "$Bx{e}^{7x/6}$ and $Cx{e}^{-7x/6}$"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071917/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5cae1fd22b1e18b4461c39cf1037217cd3796498a0960b0386be8472969e06a" "basis" 2612 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071917/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-338853" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "$Bx{e}^{x}$ and $Cx{e}^{-x}$"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071917/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5cae1fd22b1e18b4461c39cf1037217cd3796498a0960b0386be8472969e06a" "basis" 2612 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071917/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-338904" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-12\\lambda {y}^{′}+36{\\lambda }^{2}y=0,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=0,\\,y(1)+2{y}^{′}(1)=0,\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\lambda \\in R.\n$$\n\nFind the eigenvalues and eigenfunctions of this problem. Assume that $B$ and $C$ are constants.$\\,$ The eigenvalues are $\\lambda =$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The eigenfunctions are $y=$ {{field-2}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071917/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5cae1fd22b1e18b4461c39cf1037217cd3796498a0960b0386be8472969e06a" "basis" 2612 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071917/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-338904" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We consider the cases $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda \\ne 0$ separately, since they correspond to different solutions. \n\n**Case 1**: First, we consider $\\lambda =0.$ \n\nSetting $\\lambda =0$ gives the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny=Ax+B.\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions ${y}^{′}(0)=0$ and $y(1)+2{y}^{′}(1)=0$ yields the trivial solution $y≡0.$ Since the zero solution is not an eigenfunction, $\\lambda =0$ is not an eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider $\\lambda \\ne 0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}-12\\lambda m+36{\\lambda }^{2} & =0 \\\\ (m-6\\lambda )(m-6\\lambda ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the double root $m=6\\lambda .$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{6\\lambda x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)=x{e}^{6\\lambda x}\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{6\\lambda x}+Bx{e}^{6\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =6A\\lambda {e}^{6\\lambda x}+B{e}^{6\\lambda x}+6B\\lambda x{e}^{6\\lambda x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition ${y}^{′}(0)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6A\\lambda +B & =0 \\\\ (6\\lambda )A+B & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(1)+2{y}^{′}(1)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{6\\lambda }+B{e}^{6\\lambda }+2(6A\\lambda {e}^{6\\lambda }+B{e}^{6\\lambda }+6B\\lambda {e}^{6\\lambda }) & =0 \\\\ A+B+2(6A\\lambda +B+6B\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ A+B+12A\\lambda +2B+12B\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ (1+12\\lambda )A+(3+12\\lambda )B & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following systems of equations for $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(6\\lambda )A+B=0 \\\\ (1+12\\lambda )A+(3+12\\lambda )B=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis homogeneous linear system has a nontrivial solution if and only if the determinant is zero. This gives\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}6\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ 1+12\\lambda  & 3+12\\lambda \\end{aligned}|=0.\n$$\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6\\lambda (3+12\\lambda )-(1)(1+12\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ 18\\lambda +72{\\lambda }^{2}-1-12\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ 72{\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda -1 & =0 \\\\ (12\\lambda -1)(6\\lambda +1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-\\frac{1}{6}\\text{ and }{\\lambda }_{2}=\\frac{1}{12}.$\n\nFinally, we find the eigenfunction corresponding to each eigenvalue:\n\n- Consider ${\\lambda }_{1}=-\\frac{1}{6}.$ Then, we have the solution\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}(x) & =A{e}^{6{\\lambda }_{1}x}+Bx{e}^{6{\\lambda }_{1}x} \\\\  & =A{e}^{-x}+Bx{e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting ${\\lambda }_{1}$ into\n\n$$\n(6\\lambda )A+B=0\n$$\n\n \nwe get \n\n$$\n-A+B=0\\,⇒\\,A=B\n$$\n\nwhere $B$ is a free parameter. Thus, the eigenfunction corresponding to this eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=B{e}^{-x}(x+1).\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=\\frac{1}{12},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}(x) & =A{e}^{6{\\lambda }_{2}x}+Bx{e}^{6{\\lambda }_{2}x} \\\\  & =A{e}^{x/2}+Bx{e}^{x/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting ${\\lambda }_{2}$ into\n\n$$\n(6\\lambda )A+B=0\n$$\n\n \nwe get \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}A+B=0\\,⇒\\,A=-2B\n$$\n\nwhere $B$ is a free parameter. Thus, the eigenfunction corresponding to this eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}(x) & =-2B{e}^{x/2}+Bx{e}^{x/2} \\\\  & =B{e}^{x/2}(x-2) \\\\  & =C{e}^{x/2}(x-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we have set $C=B.$\n\nSo, the eigenfunctions corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively, are $B{e}^{-x}(x+1)\\text{ and }C{e}^{x/2}(x-2).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071917/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5cae1fd22b1e18b4461c39cf1037217cd3796498a0960b0386be8472969e06a" "basis" 2612 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071917/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-338904" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071917/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "2c72764fbd932c6506ae2af080dac9b2ff7abceca44e7e85139d6000ba16c77e" "basis" 2612 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071917/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-338904" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/referen