loper/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071917/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-338900" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "$Bx{e}^{-2x}$ and $C{e}^{x}(x-3)$"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071917/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5cae1fd22b1e18b4461c39cf1037217cd3796498a0960b0386be8472969e06a" "basis" 2612 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071917/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-338900" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "$Bx{e}^{-x}$ and $C{e}^{x}(x-1)$"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071917/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5cae1fd22b1e18b4461c39cf1037217cd3796498a0960b0386be8472969e06a" "basis" 2612 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071917/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-338900" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "$B{e}^{-x}(x+1)$ and $C{e}^{x/2}(x-2)$"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071917/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5cae1fd22b1e18b4461c39cf1037217cd3796498a0960b0386be8472969e06a" "basis" 2612 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071917/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-338900" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "$B{e}^{-x}(x-1)$ and $C{e}^{x/2}(x-2)$"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071917/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5cae1fd22b1e18b4461c39cf1037217cd3796498a0960b0386be8472969e06a" "basis" 2612 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071917/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-338900" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$B{e}^{-x}(x+1)$ and $C{e}^{x/2}(x-2)$"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071917/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5cae1fd22b1e18b4461c39cf1037217cd3796498a0960b0386be8472969e06a" "basis" 2612 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071917/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-338920" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+16\\lambda y=0,\\,y(0)=y(\\pi )=0,\\,0\\le x\\le \\pi ,\\,\\lambda \\ge 0.\n$$\n\nFind the parameter values corresponding to the eigenvalues and the associated eigenfunctions of this problem.$\\,$ The eigenvalues correspond to the parameter values ${\\lambda }_{n}=$ {{field-1}}, where $n\\in N.$\n\n$\\,$ The eigenfunctions are ${y}_{n}(x)=$ {{field-2}}, where ${B}_{n}\\in R.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071917/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5cae1fd22b1e18b4461c39cf1037217cd3796498a0960b0386be8472969e06a" "basis" 2612 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071917/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-338920" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We consider the cases $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda >0$ separately, since they correspond to different solutions. \n\n**Case 1**: First, we consider $\\lambda =0.$ \n\nSetting $\\lambda =0$ gives the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny=Ax+B.\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions $y(0)=y(\\pi )=0$ yields the trivial solution $y≡0.$ Since the zero solution is not an eigenfunction, $\\lambda =0$ does not correspond to an eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider $\\lambda >0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}+16\\lambda  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $m=\\pm 4\\text{i}\\sqrt{\\lambda }.$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x\\sqrt{\\lambda }),\\,{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x\\sqrt{\\lambda })\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x\\sqrt{\\lambda })+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x\\sqrt{\\lambda }).\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(0)=0$ yields\n\n$$\nA\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)=0\n$$\n\nwhich gives $A=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\ny=B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x\\sqrt{\\lambda }).\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(\\pi )=0$ yields\n\n$$\nB\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\pi \\sqrt{\\lambda })=0.\n$$\n\nFor nontrivial solutions, we require \n\n$$\n4\\pi \\sqrt{\\lambda }=n\\pi \n$$\n\nfor $n\\in N.$ This gives the parameter values corresponding to the eigenvalues:\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{n}=\\frac{{n}^{2}}{16}.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding eigenfunctions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{n}(x)={B}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{n}}),\\,{B}_{n}\\in R.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071917/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5cae1fd22b1e18b4461c39cf1037217cd3796498a0960b0386be8472969e06a" "basis" 2612 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071917/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-338920" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Develope