d" "field-2" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{n}})"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071917/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5cae1fd22b1e18b4461c39cf1037217cd3796498a0960b0386be8472969e06a" "basis" 2612 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071917/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-338920" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{n}})"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071917/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5cae1fd22b1e18b4461c39cf1037217cd3796498a0960b0386be8472969e06a" "basis" 2612 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071917/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-338920" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{n}})"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071917/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5cae1fd22b1e18b4461c39cf1037217cd3796498a0960b0386be8472969e06a" "basis" 2612 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071917/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-338667" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4{\\lambda }^{2}y=0,\\,y(0)=y(1)=0,\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\lambda \\ge 0.\n$$\n\nFind the parameter values corresponding to the eigenvalues and the associated eigenfunctions of this problem.\n$\\,$ The eigenvalues correspond to the parameter values ${\\lambda }_{n}=$ {{field-1}}, where $n\\in N.$\n\n$\\,$ The eigenfunctions are ${y}_{n}(x)=$ {{field-2}}, where ${B}_{n}\\in R.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071917/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5cae1fd22b1e18b4461c39cf1037217cd3796498a0960b0386be8472969e06a" "basis" 2612 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071917/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-338667" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We consider the cases $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda \\ne 0$ separately, since they correspond to different solutions. \n\n**Case 1**: First, we consider $\\lambda =0.$ \n\nSetting $\\lambda =0$ gives the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny=Ax+B.\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions $y(0)=y(1)=0$ yields the trivial solution $y≡0.$ Since the zero solution is not an eigenfunction, $\\lambda =0$ does not correspond to an eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider $\\lambda >0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}+4{\\lambda }^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $m=\\pm 2\\text{i}\\lambda .$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\lambda x),\\,{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\lambda x)\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\lambda x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\lambda x).\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(0)=0$ yields\n\n$$\nA\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)=0\n$$\n\nwhich gives $A=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\ny=B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\lambda x).\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(1)=0$ yields\n\n$$\nB\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\lambda )=0.\n$$\n\nFor nontrivial solutions, we require \n\n$$\n2\\lambda =n\\pi \n$$\n\nfor $n\\in N.$ This gives the parameter values corresponding to the eigenvalues:\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{n}=\\frac{n\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding eigenfunctions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{n}(x)={B}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2{\\lambda }_{n}x),\\,{B}_{n}\\in R.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071917/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5cae1fd22b1e18b4461c39cf1037217cd3796498a0960b0386be8472969e06a" "basis" 2612 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071917/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-338667" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071917/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "2c72764fbd932c6506ae2af080dac9b2ff7abceca44e7e85139d6000ba16c77e" "basis" 2612 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071917/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-338667" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071917/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5cae1fd22b1e18b4461c39cf1037217cd3796498a0960b0386be8472969e06a" "basis" 2612 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071917/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-338667" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "2n"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071917/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5cae1fd22b1e18b4461c39cf1037217cd3796498a0960b0386be8472969e06a" "basis" 2612 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/referenc