hacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071917/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-338915" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{n}})"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071917/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5cae1fd22b1e18b4461c39cf1037217cd3796498a0960b0386be8472969e06a" "basis" 2612 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071917/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-338915" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{n}})"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071917/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5cae1fd22b1e18b4461c39cf1037217cd3796498a0960b0386be8472969e06a" "basis" 2612 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071917/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-338915" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{n}})"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071917/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5cae1fd22b1e18b4461c39cf1037217cd3796498a0960b0386be8472969e06a" "basis" 2612 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071917/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-21808" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\ny'' - 18\\lambda y' + 81\\lambda^2y  = 0, \\quad y'(1) = 0, \\quad y(2) + 2y'(2) = 0, \\quad 1 \\leq x \\leq 2, \\quad \\lambda \\in\\mathbb R.\n$$\n\nFind the eigenvalues and eigenfunctions of this problem. Assume that $B$ and $C$ are constants." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071917/example-21808.json" "file_sha256" "f647803723b2ccd99be0dc27395519b11d407305f78cdb919b19825087a629e2" "basis" 2612 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071917/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-21808" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We consider the cases $\\lambda = 0$ and $\\lambda \\neq 0$ separately, since they correspond to different solutions. \n\n**Case 1**: First, we consider $\\lambda = 0.$ \n\nSetting $\\lambda = 0$ gives the equation\n\n$$\ny'' = 0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny = Ax + B.\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions $y'(1) =0$ and $y(2) + 2y'(2) = 0$ yields the trivial solution $y\\equiv 0.$ Since the zero solution is not an eigenfunction, $\\lambda = 0$ is not an eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider $\\lambda \\neq 0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y = e^{m x}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}-18\\lambda m+81{\\lambda }^{2} & =0 \\\\ (m-9\\lambda )(m-9\\lambda ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the double root $m = 9\\lambda.$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\ny_1(x) = e^{9\\lambda x}, \\qquad y_2(x) = xe^{9\\lambda x}\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny = Ae^{9\\lambda x} + Bxe^{9\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =9A\\lambda {e}^{9\\lambda x}+B{e}^{9\\lambda x}+9B\\lambda x{e}^{9\\lambda x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y'(1) = 0$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9A\\lambda {e}^{9\\lambda }+B{e}^{9\\lambda }+9B\\lambda {e}^{9\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ 9A\\lambda +B+9B\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ (9\\lambda )A+(1+9\\lambda )B & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(2) + 2y'(2) = 0$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{18\\lambda }+2B{e}^{18\\lambda }+2(9A\\lambda {e}^{18\\lambda }+B{e}^{18\\lambda }+18B\\lambda {e}^{18\\lambda }) & =0 \\\\ A+2B+2(9A\\lambda +B+18B\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ A+2B+18A\\lambda +2B+36B\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ (1+18\\lambda )A+(4+36\\lambda )B & =0 \\\\ (1+18\\lambda )A+4(1+9\\lambda )B & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following systems of equations for $A$ and $B{:}$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(9\\lambda )A+(1+9\\lambda )B=0 \\\\ (1+18\\lambda )A+4(1+9\\lambda )B=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis homogeneous linear system has a nontrivial solution if and only if the determinant is zero. This gives\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071917/assets/29df2dce71db62482d1664cc2f4033dce94574fb1903d80828f4f1d367f8c9a9.png)\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9\\lambda \\cdot 4(1+9\\lambda )-(1+9\\lambda )(1+18\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ (1+9\\lambda )[36\\lambda -(1+18\\lambda )] & =0 \\\\ (1+9\\lambda )[36\\lambda -1-18\\lambda ] & =0 \\\\ (1+9\\lambda )(18\\lambda -1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have the eigenvalues $\\lambda_1 = -\\dfrac19  \\textrm{ and } \\lambda_2 = \\dfrac1{18}.$\n\nFinally, we find the eigenfunction corresponding to each eigenvalue:\n\n- Consider $\\lambda_1 = -\\dfrac19.$ Then, we have the solution\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}(x) & =A{e}^{9{\\lambda }_{1}x}+Bx{e}^{9{\\lambda }_{1}x} \\\\  & =A{e}^{-x}+Bx{e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting $\\lambda_1$ into\n\n$$\n(9\\lambda)A  + (1 + 9\\lambda)B =0\n$$\n\n \nwe get \n\n$$\n-A  + \\left(1 -1\\right)B =0\n$$\n\n \nwhich gives $A = 0,$ and where $B$ is a free parameter. Thus, the eigenfunction corresponding to this eigenvalue is\n\n$$\ny_1(x) = Bxe^{-x}.\n$$\n\n- For $\\lambda_2 = \\dfrac1{18},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}(x) & =A{e}^{9{\\lambda }_{2}x}+Bx{e}^{9{\\lambda }_{2}x} \\\\  & =A{e}^{x/2}+Bx{e}^{x/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting $\\lambda_2$ into\n\n$$\n(9\\lambda)A  + (1 + 9\\lambda)B =0\n$$\n\n \nwe get \n\n$$\n\\dfrac12A + \\dfrac32 B = 0 \\quad \\Rightarrow\\quad A = -3B\n$$\n\nwhere $B$ is a free parameter. Thus, the eigenfunction corresponding to this eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}(x) & =-3B{e}^{x/2}+Bx{e}^{x/2} \\\\  & =B{e}^{x/2}(x-3) \\\\  & =C{e}^{x/2}(x-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we have set $C = B.$\n\nSo, the eigenfunctions corresponding to $\\lambda_1$ and $\\lambda_2,$ respectively, are $Bxe^{-x} \\textrm{ and } Ce^{x/2}(x-3).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071917/example-21808.json" "file_sha256" "f647803723b2ccd99be0dc27395519b11d407305f78cdb919b19825087a629e2" "basis" 2612 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071917/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-21806" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\ny'' + 9\\lambda y = 0, \\qquad y(0) = y(\\pi) = 0, \\quad 0 \\leq x \\leq \\pi, \\quad \\lambda \\geq 0.\n$$\n\nFind the eigenvalues and eigenfunctions of this problem." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071917/example-21806.json" "file_sha256" "3fa18bfee64ad891246e6b0dd240cfe6cb2dc9e3d05f4b0cd58dbbb68b0c3ada" "basis" 2612 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071917/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-21806" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We consider the cases $\\lambda = 0$ and $\\lambda > 0$ separately, since they correspond to different solutions. \n\n**Case 1**: First, we consider $\\lambda = 0.$ \n\nSetting $\\lambda = 0$ gives the equation\n\n$$\ny'' = 0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny = Ax + B.\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions $y(0) = y(\\pi) = 0$ yields the trivial solution $y\\equiv 0.$ Since the zero solution is not an eigenfunction, $\\lambda = 0$ is not an eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider $\\lambda > 0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y = e^{m x}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}+9\\lambda  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $m = \\pm 3\\textrm i\\sqrt{\\lambda} .$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\ny_1(x) = \\cos (3x\\sqrt{\\lambda}), \\qquad y_2(x) = \\sin (3x\\sqrt{\\lambda})\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny = A\\cos (3x\\sqrt{\\lambda}) + B\\sin (3x\\sqrt{\\lambda}).\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(0) = 0$ yields\n\n$$\nA\\cos (0) + B\\sin (0) = 0\n$$\n\nwhich gives $A = 0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\ny = B\\sin (3x\\sqrt{\\lambda}).\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(\\pi) = 0$ yields\n\n$$\nB\\sin (3\\pi\\sqrt{\\lambda}) = 0.\n$$\n\nFor nontrivial solutions, we require \n\n$$\n3\\pi\\sqrt{\\lambda} = n\\pi\n$$\n\nfor $n\\in \\mathbb N.$ This gives the set of eigenvalues\n\n$$\n\\lambda_n = \\dfrac{n^2}{9}.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding eigenfunctions are \n\n$$\ny_n(x) = B_n \\sin(3x\\sqrt{\\lambda_n}), \\qquad B_n \\in\\mathbb R.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071917/example-21806.json" "file_sha256" "3fa18bfee64ad891246e6b0dd240cfe6cb2dc9e3d05f4b0cd58dbbb68b0c3ada" "basis" 2612 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071917/edb-import/verification.json"}] "sources" [] "usage" {"q-338667" [] "q-338729" [] "q-338904" [] "q-338900" [] "e-21806" [] "q-338915" [] "q-338920" [] "e-21808" [] "q-338921" [] "q-338905" [] "q-338916" [] "q-338853" []} "basis" 2615 "retractions" [] "immutable_answers" [17592186164743 17592186164744 17592186164745 17592186164746 17592186164747 1759